{
  "id": "latin",
  "entries": [
    {
      "id": "at-a-glance",
      "topic": "overview",
      "title": "Latin at a glance",
      "body": [
        "Latin shows who did what with word endings rather than word order. Nouns change their ending for case and number, verbs for person, number, tense, mood and voice. Because the endings carry the grammar, words can be moved around for emphasis; the verb most often comes last.",
        "There are no articles: puella can mean a girl or the girl. Nouns have three genders, masculine, feminine and neuter."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "Puella {canem} videt.",
          "e": "The girl sees the dog."
        },
        {
          "t": "{Canem} puella videt.",
          "e": "The girl sees the dog. (same meaning: canem is still the object)"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "inflection",
        "word order"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pronunciation",
      "topic": "sounds",
      "title": "Classical and Church pronunciation",
      "body": [
        "Classical pronunciation reconstructs how educated Romans spoke around the 1st century BC. Ecclesiastical (Church) pronunciation follows modern Italian and is used in choral music and the Catholic Church.",
        "Vowels can be short or long; long ones are marked with a macron in textbooks (ā, ē, ī, ō, ū) and are held about twice as long."
      ],
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Spelling",
          "Classical",
          "Ecclesiastical"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "c",
            "always k: Cicerō = KI-ke-ro",
            "ch before e, i, ae, oe: CHI-che-ro"
          ],
          [
            "g",
            "always hard g",
            "soft (as in gem) before e, i"
          ],
          [
            "v",
            "w: vēnī = WAY-nee",
            "v"
          ],
          [
            "ae",
            "as in eye",
            "as in bet"
          ],
          [
            "ti + vowel",
            "ti",
            "tsi: nātiō = NAH-tsi-o"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "tags": [
        "pronunciation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "stress",
      "topic": "sounds",
      "title": "Where the stress falls",
      "body": "Words of two syllables are stressed on the first. In longer words, stress the second-to-last syllable if it is heavy (it has a long vowel or ends in a consonant); otherwise stress the third-to-last.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "a{mī}cus",
          "e": "friend · -mī- is long, so it takes the stress"
        },
        {
          "t": "{dō}minus",
          "e": "master · -mi- is short and open, so the stress moves back"
        },
        {
          "t": "pu{el}la",
          "e": "girl · -el- ends in a consonant, so it is heavy"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "pronunciation",
        "stress"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cases",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "The cases",
      "body": "A noun's case shows its job in the sentence. Latin has six main cases, plus a locative for the names of towns and a few other words (Rōmae, at Rome; domī, at home).",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Case",
          "Job",
          "Example"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Nominative",
            "subject; complement of to be",
            "{Puella} cantat. · The girl sings."
          ],
          [
            "Genitive",
            "of; possession",
            "liber {puellae} · the girl's book"
          ],
          [
            "Dative",
            "to, for; indirect object",
            "{Puellae} librum dō. · I give the girl a book."
          ],
          [
            "Accusative",
            "direct object; motion towards",
            "{Puellam} videō. · I see the girl."
          ],
          [
            "Ablative",
            "by, with, from, in; means and time",
            "{Puellā} cantante · while the girl sings"
          ],
          [
            "Vocative",
            "addressing someone",
            "{Mārce}, venī! · Marcus, come!"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "tags": [
        "grammar",
        "cases"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "declensions",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "The five declensions",
      "body": "Nouns fall into five patterns of endings, called declensions. Dictionaries list the nominative and the genitive singular; the genitive ending tells you the declension and gives the stem.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Declension",
          "Genitive sg.",
          "Examples",
          "Usual gender"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "1st",
            "-ae",
            "puella, puellae · girl; nauta, nautae · sailor",
            "feminine"
          ],
          [
            "2nd",
            "-ī",
            "dominus, dominī · master; bellum, bellī · war",
            "masculine, neuter"
          ],
          [
            "3rd",
            "-is",
            "rēx, rēgis · king; corpus, corporis · body",
            "any"
          ],
          [
            "4th",
            "-ūs",
            "manus, manūs · hand; cornū, cornūs · horn",
            "masculine"
          ],
          [
            "5th",
            "-ēī / -eī",
            "rēs, reī · thing; diēs, diēī · day",
            "feminine"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "tags": [
        "nouns",
        "declensions"
      ]
    },
