    {
      "id": "conjugations",
      "topic": "verbs",
      "title": "The four conjugations",
      "body": [
        "Verbs fall into four conjugations, told apart by the infinitive: -āre, -ēre, -ere and -īre. A fifth mixed group (capiō, capere) takes some third and some fourth conjugation endings.",
        "Dictionaries give four principal parts: first person present, infinitive, first person perfect and supine. Learn all four, because the perfect and supine stems are often unpredictable."
      ],
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Conjugation",
          "Principal parts",
          "Meaning"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "1st -āre",
            "amō, amāre, amāvī, amātum",
            "love"
          ],
          [
            "2nd -ēre",
            "moneō, monēre, monuī, monitum",
            "warn"
          ],
          [
            "3rd -ere",
            "regō, regere, rēxī, rēctum",
            "rule"
          ],
          [
            "3rd -iō",
            "capiō, capere, cēpī, captum",
            "take"
          ],
          [
            "4th -īre",
            "audiō, audīre, audīvī, audītum",
            "hear"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "tags": [
        "verbs",
        "conjugation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "present-tense",
      "topic": "verbs",
      "title": "Present tense endings",
      "body": "The personal endings -ō (or -m), -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt are the same across almost every active tense. The subject pronoun is usually left out because the ending already shows it.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
