    {
      "id": "agreement",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "Adjective agreement",
      "body": "An adjective matches its noun in gender, number and case, but not necessarily in declension, so the endings don't always look alike. Adjectives usually follow the noun.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "puella {bona}",
          "e": "a good girl (feminine)"
        },
        {
          "t": "dominus {bonus}",
          "e": "a good master (masculine)"
        },
        {
          "t": "bellum {bonum}",
          "e": "a good war (neuter)"
        },
        {
          "t": "nauta {bonus}",
          "e": "a good sailor: nauta is 1st declension but masculine"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "adjectives",
        "agreement"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "prepositions",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "Prepositions and their cases",
      "body": "Each preposition takes a set case. In and sub take the ablative for place where, and the accusative for motion into.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "+ accusative",
          "+ ablative"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "ad · to, towards",
            "ā, ab · from, by"
          ],
          [
            "per · through",
            "cum · with"
          ],
          [
            "post · after, behind",
            "dē · down from, about"
          ],
          [
            "ante · before",
            "ē, ex · out of"
          ],
          [
            "trāns · across",
            "sine · without"
          ],
          [
            "in · into",
            "in · in, on"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "in {urbe}",
          "e": "in the city (ablative)"
        },
        {
          "t": "in {urbem}",
          "e": "into the city (accusative)"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "prepositions",
        "cases"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "numbers",
      "topic": "numbers",
      "title": "Numbers and numerals",
      "body": [
        "ūnus, duo, trēs, quattuor, quīnque, sex, septem, octō, novem, decem. Only ūnus, duo and trēs change their endings to agree with a noun.",
        "Roman numerals use I 1, V 5, X 10, L 50, C 100, D 500, M 1000. A smaller numeral before a larger one is subtracted: IV 4, IX 9, XL 40, XC 90, CD 400, CM 900."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "MMXXVI",
          "e": "2026"
        },
        {
          "t": "MCMXCIX",
          "e": "1999 · M + CM + XC + IX"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "numbers",
        "roman numerals"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrases",
      "topic": "phrases",
      "title": "Greetings and phrases",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "Salvē! · Salvēte!",
          "e": "Hello! (to one · to several)"
        },
        {
          "t": "Valē! · Valēte!",
          "e": "Goodbye! (to one · to several)"
        },
        {
          "t": "Quid agis?",
          "e": "How are you doing?"
        },
        {
          "t": "Bene mē habeō.",
          "e": "I'm well."
        },
        {
          "t": "Grātiās tibi agō.",
          "e": "Thank you."
        },
        {
          "t": "Quōmodo tibi nōmen est?",
          "e": "What is your name?"
        },
        {
          "t": "Mihi nōmen est Marcus.",
          "e": "My name is Marcus."
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "greetings"
      ]
    },
